  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "Uses SAM’s sharpness and generalization motivation to develop Sparse SAM, and reports its performance; it does not test any listed claim version.",
    "gate_note": "The study evaluates Sparse SAM against SAM and SGD. Its gradient-difference analysis, landscape visualizations, and Hessian spectra are used to motivate or characterize its method; they do not measure the magnitude-aware sharpness and train-test-gap relationship named in V7. Results cited from other work are not extracted.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses the flatness–generalization motivation to develop Sparse SAM and evaluates its optimizer performance. Its filter-normalized loss landscapes and Hessian spectra do not measure V7's magnitude-aware perturbation tolerance against train–test gaps or compare gap rankings with norm and margin measures. No other version's intervention or comparison is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record correctly captures the authors' conclusion that SSAM can match or outperform SAM and has the same proved asymptotic convergence rate under their assumptions. Its efficiency wording needs qualification: FLOP savings are theoretical estimates, and Appendix C explicitly says their masks lack an implemented sparse operation delivering training acceleration. Actual end-to-end acceleration is potential, not demonstrated.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Introduces Fisher-information and dynamic-sparse masks for SAM perturbations; trains image classifiers with data augmentation and reports test accuracy. It does not use the claim versions’ interventions or measurements.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Dataset, model, SSAM variant, sparsity ratio, Fisher sample count, and mask-update interval affect reported performance; higher sparsity is reported to cause more obvious drops on ImageNet than on CIFAR.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "At 50% sparsity, SSAM's accuracy differences from SAM range from -0.44 to +0.96 percentage points across the selected model–dataset comparisons. Some higher-sparsity settings lose accuracy.",
        "proposed_claim": "Sparse SAM can preserve or improve held-out accuracy relative to SAM, depending on architecture, dataset, mask method, and sparsity.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R3",
          "R4",
          "R7",
          "R8",
          "R11",
          "R12",
          "R13",
          "R14",
          "R17",
          "R18",
          "R19",
          "R20",
          "R23",
          "R24",
          "R25",
          "R26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "For ResNet18 on CIFAR10, the authors describe SSAM's filter-normalized training-loss landscape as flatter. Dominant Hessian eigenvalues are 82.6 for SGD, 26.2 for SAM, and 19.4 for SSAM; corresponding first-to-fifth eigenvalue ratios are 16.9, 2.0, and 1.7.",
        "proposed_claim": "Sparse SAM can produce flatter visualized training-loss landscapes and smaller dominant Hessian eigenvalues than SGD or SAM in this comparison.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "On ImageNet, random masking reaches 77.08%, dropping large-gradient weights 76.68%, and dropping small-gradient weights 77.25%, against SAM's 77.25%.",
        "proposed_claim": "The selection of parameters for sparse perturbation affects Sparse SAM's held-out accuracy.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R27",
          "R28",
          "R29"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/Mi-Peng/Sparse-Sharpness-Aware-Minimization",
    "peer_reviewed": "Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2022)",
    "problems": "The authors report no error bars across random seeds and no compute-resource totals. Their stated limitation says there is currently no concrete implemented sparse operation to achieve training acceleration. The supplied text does not report train-test gaps or random-label experiments.",
    "rows_left_out": "Tables 1–3 contain additional sparsity conditions for each model and dataset, and Appendix Tables 4–6, 8–9 contain further accuracy and hyperparameter results. The selected rows retain baseline comparisons, 50% sparsity, and representative high-sparsity conditions across the main datasets; the remaining sparsity levels and appendix results were omitted for the 60-row limit and because none maps to a listed version.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "Random-label fitting and held-out performance compared with natural-label training."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "A width sweep beyond the zero-training-error threshold; comparing ResNet and WideResNet does not supply this."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "Convolutional versus fully connected classifiers and the contribution of local connectivity."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Adaptive optimizers versus SGD under limited or broader tuning; SAM and SSAM are not the adaptive-gradient comparison named here."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Batch-size effects under matched epoch budgets and adaptations that reduce large-batch penalties."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Dropout interventions, training-set-size dependence, and joint removal of dropout and weight decay."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "Magnitude-aware perturbation tolerance correlated with train–test gaps at similarly low training loss, including comparisons with norm and margin measures. Landscape plots and Hessian spectra alone do not establish this."
      },
      {
        "version": "V8",
        "parts": "Removing high-memorization examples versus equally many random examples and retraining."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "All recorded accuracies are outside the listed versions; cannot_determine does not indicate unreadable numbers. The rows should be excluded from claim-test evidence. Excerpt E2 reproduces an overbroad author statement: Table 1 shows 50%-sparse SSAM-F below SAM on CIFAR10 and SSAM-D below SAM on CIFAR100. Its attached R11, R17, and R23 concern WideResNet or ResNet50, not the ResNet18 named in the excerpt. The Appendix C limitation is present in the supplied text despite the record's failed excerpt match."
